# PHASE 3 — WORLD-CLASS PRODUCT IMAGE PRESENTATION

A major customer complaint is that some product photographs are incomplete, too small or insufficient to show what the customer is actually buying.

This must be corrected.

## 3.1 Product card images

The product listing cards should display:

- Clear, high-quality product photographs.
- Consistent image dimensions and presentation.
- A complete view of the garment wherever possible.
- Product name.
- Correct price.
- Available colour indicators where relevant.
- A clear indication of available sizes where appropriate.
- A visible link to view the full product.

Do not crop garments in a way that hides important parts of the design.

Do not cut off the hem, sleeves, hood, neckline or other important details.

Use a consistent image container, but preserve the full garment using an appropriate image fit such as `contain` when necessary.

Avoid forcing every photograph into an unsuitable crop.

## 3.2 Detailed product image gallery

Every product detail page must have a professional image gallery.

The gallery should support:

1. Large primary image.
2. Clickable thumbnail images.
3. Front view.
4. Back view.
5. Side view.
6. Close-up photographs.
7. Fabric and texture details.
8. Additional product photographs.
9. Full-screen image viewing.
10. Image zoom.
11. Easy previous/next image navigation.

The gallery should be responsive on mobile and desktop.

Customers should be able to inspect the garment comfortably without downloading the image.

The image gallery must show only photographs belonging to the selected product and, where applicable, its selected colour variation.

## 3.3 Do not manufacture missing product photographs

Use genuine photographs supplied or approved by the business owner.

Do not invent back views or side views using AI and present them as actual product photographs.

Do not use Google-downloaded images as though they are genuine Teefahs stock.

Do not substitute a photograph of a similar garment.

If a product has only one genuine photograph, display that photograph properly and show only the available image count. The admin dashboard should allow additional views to be added later.

Where a product has insufficient photographs, flag it as needing more images.

Do not block the entire catalogue because some products have incomplete galleries.

## 3.4 AI-enhanced and illustrative photographs

If AI-generated or AI-enhanced model images are used, they must not misrepresent the actual garment.

Provide an appropriate label such as:

- Actual Product Photograph.
- AI-Enhanced Presentation.
- Illustrative Model Image.

Use these labels only when they accurately describe the image.

The actual garment's design, colour, fabric appearance, embellishments and finishing must not be falsely represented.

The customer must be able to distinguish an illustrative presentation from a genuine photograph of the product being sold.

