# PHASE 6 — INTERNATIONAL SIZING AND MEASUREMENT SYSTEM

This is essential for international customers.

A customer in the UK, USA, Canada or another country should be able to understand whether a garment will fit before ordering.

## 6.1 Country and sizing-system information

Support relevant sizing systems, including:

- Nigerian sizing.
- UK sizing.
- US sizing.
- European sizing.
- UAE sizing.
- Egyptian sizing.

Only display the systems that are genuinely relevant to the product and supported by verified measurements.

Do not assume that a UK Large, US Large, Egyptian Large and UAE Large are identical.

Where a supplier uses a particular sizing standard, record that accurately.

## 6.2 Actual garment measurements

Allow the owner to enter measurements for each product or size variation.

Relevant measurements may include:

| Measurement | Unit |
|---|---|
| Bust/chest | cm and inches |
| Garment length | cm and inches |
| Sleeve length | cm and inches |
| Shoulder width | cm and inches |
| Waist | cm and inches |
| Hip | cm and inches |
| Other relevant dimensions | cm and inches |

Not every garment needs every measurement.

The fields should adapt to the product type.

For Abayas, garment length, bust, sleeve and shoulder measurements are particularly useful.

For Khimars, relevant length, width and coverage measurements may be more appropriate.

For Hijabs and veils, dimensions may be more useful than conventional clothing sizes.

## 6.3 Size guide on the product page

Provide an easy-to-open size guide.

It should explain:

- What each size means.
- Whether measurements refer to the body or the garment.
- How to measure correctly.
- Whether the garment has a loose or fitted cut.
- Whether customers should allow additional room for a comfortable fit.

Provide cm/inches conversion.

Do not use random generic size charts for every product.

The owner must be able to supply and edit product-specific measurements.

## 6.4 Fit guidance

Where sufficient verified measurements exist, provide helpful fit guidance.

For example:

"Compare your measurements with the garment measurements before selecting a size."

Do not guarantee that a particular size will fit a customer without sufficient information.

Do not make customers believe that a size recommendation is a professional body-fitting assessment.

